# Test coverage
coverage/

# Playwright end-to-end specs. They are NOT application code: the runner stage
# copies only .next/standalone, .next/static and public, so nothing under e2e/ has
# ever reached the shipped image. It was only ever pulled into the BUILDER by
# `COPY . .`, where it cost context transfer and busted the COPY cache layer on
# every spec edit.
#
# NO TYPE-CHECK COVERAGE IS LOST HERE — measured 2026-08-07, because the obvious
# assumption is wrong. tsconfig.json includes `**/*.ts`, so it is tempting to
# conclude that `next build` was type-checking these specs and that ignoring them
# gives that up. It was not:
#
#   planted `const broken: number = "..."` in frontend/e2e/zz-typeerror.spec.ts
#     npm run build (next build)  -> rc=0, spec not mentioned   <- does NOT check e2e
#     npx tsc --noEmit            -> rc=2, TS2322 on that line   <- does check it
#
# `next build` type-checks the pages/app graph, not every file the tsconfig
# program contains. So the Docker build never checked these specs either, and CI's
# "Run frontend build (validates TypeScript)" step does not check them today.
# That gap is PRE-EXISTING and is not created, widened or fixed by this file —
# recorded here only so the next reader does not "restore" coverage that was never
# there. Playwright itself transpiles specs without full type-checking.
#
# Excluding them keeps test code out of a production build context and lets
# scripts/check-runtime-freshness.sh stop reporting DRIFT for a spec-only commit
# that cannot change a byte of the served bundle.
#
# playwright.config.ts is excluded for the same reason and to make the rule whole:
# with only e2e/ ignored, editing the suite's projects/grepInvert still dated the
# frontend image, so a test-only change could still demand a production rebuild.
e2e/
playwright.config.ts

# Logs
*.log

# Temporary files
tmp/
temp/

# Git
.git/
.gitignore
